But be careful about year 0 or huge years: `Date.UTC` handles 0-99 by mapping to 1900+year for years less than 100 (setFullYear handles 0-99 by mapping to 1900+year for years less than 100; Date.UTC likewise). Year "0000" → treated as 1900. Leap year check would be off. Edge case: year 0000 — is 2000 a leap year? Actually year 0 is a leap year (divisible by 400). 1900 is not a leap year. So "0000-02-29T00:00:00Z" — a real moment? Yes, year 0 is a leap year in the proleptic Gregorian calendar. My check would say Feb 29, 1900 → invalid. Hmm. Also "0999-02-29" — year 999 is not a leap year either way (999 % 4 != 0). "0996-02-29" — 996 is a leap year, but gets mapped to 1996, which is also a leap year. OK. The truly troublesome case is years 0000-0099. Year 0: leap. 1900: not leap. Also 0900 → 1900: 900 is not leap, 1900 is not leap. 0004 → 1904 is leap, 4 is leap. The only discrepancy is year 0000 (leap) → 1900 (not leap). What about 0000-02-29? Does it name a real moment on the calendar? Technically in the proleptic Gregorian calendar, year 0 is a leap year. Extremely edge case. Let's avoid the Date.UTC quirk and compute daysInMonth directly:

```js
function daysInMonth(year, month) {
	if (month === 2) {
		const leap = (year % 4 === 0 && year % 100 !== 0) || year % 400 === 0;
		return leap ? 29 : 28;
	}
	return [31, 28, 31, 30, 31, 30, 31, 31, 30, 31, 30, 31][month - 1];
