```js
export function findConflicts(bookings) {
	if (!Array.isArray(bookings)) throw new TypeError("bookings must be an array");
	const seenIds = new Set();
	const parsed = [];
	for (const booking of bookings) {
		if (typeof booking !== "object" || booking === null || typeof booking.id !== "string" || booking.id === "" ||
			typeof booking.room !== "string" || booking.room === "" ||
			typeof booking.start !== "string" || !isValidInstant(booking.start) ||
			typeof booking.end !== "string" || !isValidInstant(booking.end)) {
			throw new TypeError("invalid booking");
		}
		if (booking.end <= booking.start) throw new RangeError(`booking ${booking.id} ends before it starts`);
		if (seenIds.has(booking.id)) throw new TypeError(`duplicate id ${booking.id}`);
		seenIds.add(booking.id);
		parsed.push({ id: booking.id, room: booking.room, start: booking.start, end: booking.end });
	}
	const conflicts = [];
	for (let i = 0; i < parsed.length; i++) {
		for (let j = i + 1; j < parsed.length; j++) {
			const a = parsed[i], b = parsed[j];
			if (a.room === b.room && a.start < b.end && b.start < a.end) {
				const pair = a.id < b.id ? [a.id, b.id] : [b.id, a.id];
				conflicts.push(pair);
			}
		}
	}
	conflicts.sort((p, q) => (p[0] < q[0] ? -1 : p[0] > q[0] ? 1 : p[1] < q[1] ? -1 : p[1] > q[1] ? 1 : 0));
	return conflicts;
}
```

Wait — R3 says "check bookings one at a time in array order, and the first problem found is the one thrown. For each booking, the order is: R1 for that booking, then the R2 end check, then the R2 duplicate id check against preceding bookings."

So for each booking, first R1 (all the field checks of that booking), then end check, then duplicate check. My loop does exactly that. Good.

R5: idA < idB is a normal string comparison. Note: since ids are unique (duplicates throw), pairs are distinct. Sort by idA, then idB. Could use default sort with localeCompare? No — `Array.prototype.sort` default is lexicographic in UTF-16 code units, which matches JS's `<` comparison for strings (both are UTF-16 code unit order). Actually, JS's `<` on strings compares by UTF-16 code units, and default sort does the same. But it's safer to write an explicit comparator as above.

Note: could `pair` already contain duplicates? Since ids are unique, pair [idA, idB] has idA ≠ idB.

R6: no mutation — we only read, push to a new array, and sort a new array. Good.

Edge: `end <= start` — end not after start → RangeError. Since both are valid strings, string comparison equals time comparison (fixed format). Good.

Overlap: strict inequality a.start < b.end && b.start < a.end. If touching: a.end == b.start → b.start < a.end is false → no conflict. Good.

Also, if `bookings` is not an array → TypeError (R1). If the element is not an object (e.g., null, string, number) → TypeError. If the booking's properties are, say, `id: 5` → TypeError. My check: typeof booking !== "object" || booking === null covers null/non-objects.

